From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: conhost: interpret the VT sequences programs write

With ENABLE_VIRTUAL_TERMINAL_PROCESSING set, Windows' console interprets the
escape sequences a program writes; Wine's conhost stored them in the screen
buffer as characters, so PowerShell 7 (PSReadLine), and every program that
colours its output the modern way, showed escape codes -- in a console
window, and split and garbled at line ends in a pseudo console.

write_console now parses them in that mode (a sequence split across writes
is kept until it is complete): cursor movement (CUU/CUD/CUF/CUB, CNL/CPL,
CHA, VPA, CUP/HVP), erasing (ED 0-3, EL, ECH), inserting and deleting (ICH,
DCH, IL, DL), scrolling (SU, SD, IND, RI), SGR (bold, underline, reverse;
16 colours, and 256-colour and RGB requests as the nearest of the 16 the
buffer holds), save/restore (DECSC/DECRC, SCP/RCP), ?25 cursor visibility,
?7 autowrap, the alternate screen (?47, ?1047, ?1049), device status
(DSR 5/6, answered as input) and attributes (DA), full reset (RIS), and the
title (OSC 0/2). The tty output keeps a background across an attribute
reset and passes underline and reverse on.

Gate: sg-shell's test/terminal-check.sh (a program's SGR 91 is drawn red,
PowerShell 7's Write-Host colours, SetConsoleTextAttribute colours).
